    "turn_idx": 99,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, I tell people absolutely adhere to it. I try to. And I do the best I possibly can to make sure if I go out, I have a mask on. Sometimes I have forgotten I'd run back to the car and get it. Things of that sort because you're just not used to everyday life of that. But it is important for us. It's important for all of our employees that we're asking to go back to work. See, I think every employee that if you're asking a person to open back up, go back to work, You should be able to test. You shouldn't expect a worker to go back into work environment to where they might be someone infected or had been infected or maybe still is and we don't know. They should be. They should have all PPEs. We've asked people to open up their parlors and salons. and hair care and all this. We have as dentists, we had dentists asking them to open up and they couldn't even protect themselves with the mask and all the gowns and things they needed. So if we're not prepared to do that, then don't be prepared to open up as if nothing happened. And if you're going to do it, then make sure if every business ought to have the right to say, if you're going to shop in my store, you have to have a mask on. So if you don't want to make it mandatory, then let the stores decide. Let the people shop to where they think they are protected, making sure that I think that the market will drive that. Politicians are afraid to stand up and say, I'm taking your right swing, put that mask on. Well, I want to save your life. I recommend you put the mask on. I'm going to put my mask on. And I'm going to go into a store that I think is protected as they are. I want me to be protected. So that basically is just the market forces and it should be done.",
